.TH PORTS 9
.SH NAME
ports \- the arm64 and riscv64 ports
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B import "kernel:arch/arm64"
.br
.B import "kernel:arch/riscv64"
.SH DESCRIPTION
Both ports run the same
.I kmain
amd64 does, self-tests and all, selected at build time by
.BR --arch=arm64 " or " --arch=riscv64
to
.IR build (8),
which QEMU's
.B virt
board serves for both.
Each supplies one
.BI arch_ arch .odin
binding of the names
.IR arch (9)
lists; see that page for the interface every port implements.
.SS arm64
Runs at EL1 on SP_EL1.
.B early_init
selects that stack pointer and turns the vector unit on before any Odin
statement runs, for the same reason amd64's does: the first struct move
compiles to a vector instruction.
.PP
The virtual memory manager uses two base registers rather than one.
.B TTBR1_EL1
always holds the kernel's root, with a 16-bit input region, and
.B TTBR0_EL1
holds a user root with a 17-bit input region, so a user address indexes
only the low 256 entries of the table it names.
A kernel thread's
.B TTBR0
points at the early boot window, so a null dereference inside the kernel
still faults.
.PP
The console is reached through a window built before there is an
allocator: four static page table entries in
.B early.odin
map the PL011's one page at its own physical address through
.BR TTBR0 ,
and
.I kmain
moves the port onto the kernel's own mapping once the VMM is up.
.PP
The interrupt controller is a GICv2, at
.B 0x0800_0000
on the
.B virt
board with
.B gic-version=2
on the QEMU command line: one controller for both the local and the
shared-peripheral roles the amd64 port splits across the local APIC and
the I/O APIC.
The timer is the ARM generic timer; its rate is the register
.BR CNTFRQ_EL0 ,
so there is nothing to calibrate against, and the EL1 physical timer
fires once per arming, re-armed on each acknowledge.
.PP
A software trap into the kernel is
.B svc #0x401
for a yield and
.B svc #0x402
for the interrupt bracket's self-test; any other
.B svc
from a program is the syscall door.
.SS riscv64
Runs in supervisor mode above OpenSBI, which is the console, the timer
and the way to reach another hart, through
.BR ecall ;
.B sbi.odin
has the four SBI calls the kernel makes.
.PP
.B ecall
from supervisor mode cannot trap into this kernel: it is the firmware's
own door, and no interrupt delegation changes that.
The kernel's synchronous self-trap is
.B ebreak
instead, with a vector number in
.BR a7 :
zero for a breakpoint, a software vector otherwise.
A program's
.B ecall
is delegated and is the syscall door.
.PP
The hart switches no stack on a trap.
.B sscratch
holds the current hart's per-CPU record while a program runs and zero
while the kernel runs; the trap entry swaps it with
.B tp
to learn which was true.
.PP
The console at
.B 0x1000_0000
is reached only through the firmware; nothing in the kernel maps it or
holds a register for it directly.
The clock rate has no register: it is the device tree property
.BR /cpus/timebase-frequency ,
which
.B early.odin
parses out of just enough of the flattened tree to find it, and a tree
it cannot parse leaves the timer refusing to calibrate.
The interrupt controller is the PLIC: source numbers from 1, a
supervisor context per hart, enable bits as a mask.
Paging is Sv48, pinned, the same four-level tree with the same address
split amd64 uses.
.PP
The vector layout: exception causes 0 through 15 are traps, interrupt
causes start at 16 so the timer is 21, PLIC sources start at 32, and the
software vectors start at
.BR 0x81 .
An
.B ebreak
carries its vector in
.BR a7 ;
a software interrupt carries none of its own, so the sender leaves a bit
set in the receiver's per-CPU record instead.
.SS Build flags per architecture
.B --arch=arm64
selects Odin target
.B freestanding_arm64
and
.BR kernel/link_arm64.ld ,
assembles
.BR vectors.S ,
.B ap.S
and
.B fpu_hold.S
under
.BR kernel/arch/arm64 ,
and boots QEMU's
.B virt
machine with
.BR gic-version=2 .
.B --arch=riscv64
selects
.B freestanding_riscv64
and
.BR kernel/link_riscv64.ld ,
assembles the matching three
.B .S
files under
.BR kernel/arch/riscv64 ,
links with
.B -z norelro
because
.B ld.lld
otherwise carves a read-only segment for the GOT out of
.BR .data ,
and boots
.B virt
with
.BR acpi=off ,
since the firmware publishes either ACPI tables or a device tree and the
clock rate is only ever in the tree.
Every procedure either port's assembly enters, or the bootloader calls,
is declared
.BR "proc ""c""" ,
the only calling convention the compiler accepts on these targets.
Every inline
.B asm
template for a system instruction with no operand form in the
compiler's checker
.RB ( "msr daifset" ,
the barriers,
.BR tlbi ,
.BR brk ,
.BR svc ,
.BR ecall ,
.BR ebreak ,
.BR sfence.vma ,
a read of the stack pointer)
is a
.B #byte
sequence with its register pinned to
.B x0
or
.BR a0 .
Both ports use UEFI firmware,
.B edk2-aarch64
and
.B edk2-riscv
respectively, the same way amd64 uses
.BR edk2-x86_64 .
.SH SOURCE
.B kernel/arch/arm64
.br
.B kernel/arch/riscv64
.br
.B build.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR arch (9),
.IR smp (9),
.IR memory (9),
.IR build (8)
.SH BUGS
Neither port has a non-maskable stop for the panic path.
The arm64 stop is a software-generated interrupt and the riscv64 stop is
an ordinary software interrupt; a core inside a spinlock takes neither
until it releases the lock, and can still overwrite a fault report in
that window.
Neither board has a PS/2 controller;
.B kbd.init
reads all-ones from the missing port and reports no keyboard, so input
on both ports is the serial line only.
The riscv64 16550 is reached only through firmware calls, never mapped
directly.
The device tree is parsed for exactly one property, and the GIC, PLIC
and UART addresses are hard-coded to the
.B virt
board's own, the same way the I/O APIC's address is assumed on amd64.
The PCI ECAM base is likewise hard-coded per board:
.B 0x4010000000
on arm64 and
.B 0x30000000
on riscv64.
.B arch.cpu_class
answers
.B .Performance
for every core on both ports; there is no big.LITTLE classification.
